Track 3: "Did You Hear the North Pier Answer?"
- Story & Context: Legend around the Severn Estuary claims that by 1989, captains of incoming supertankers would deliberately kill their engines off Portishead Point just to exchange brass signals with the warehouse parties. During the legendary winter solstice dance, three thousand ravers clad in fluorescent sou’westers danced in ankle-deep sea spray as Vance’s tuba and three offshore container horns locked into an unprompted, twenty-minute polyrhythmic celebration through the pea-soup fog. When local constables finally severed the generator cables at daybreak, the sound didn't die; the ships kept blaring the syncopated rhythm back and forth across the channel for another hour. Over the fading hiss of cooling coastal mud, the crowd screamed the triumphant refrain back into the open sea: "Two blasts for yes, love, four blasts for home!"
